#Petroleum manual -- power generation, storage, stations, deep refining
ie.manual.entry.petroleum20=A Diesel Generator is a good machine and it never changes. The end of an oil chain deserves buildings instead - plants you walk into, fed by pipe, measured in tens of thousands of Flux.<br>They are built around one axis: §6response against efficiency§r. Nothing here is simply better than everything else. The engine bank answers instantly and wastes fuel; the turbine hall is brutally efficient and hates being cycled; the gas turbine sits between them.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum21=The §6Fuel Oil Boiler§r is the furnace half of a power station, and it makes no Flux at all - it makes §6steam§r.<br>That is what finally gives §6heavy fuel oil§r a job at scale. Crude and diesel burn in it too, and both are worse trades: diesel is worth more in an engine and crude is worth more refined. Water goes in along the base course, fuel goes in with it, and steam leaves from the crown.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum22=§6Steam§r is a real fluid here, not a number hidden inside one machine. It has a bucket, it has a pipe, and it moves between buildings.<br>That is the entire reason the boiler and the turbine hall are separate structures rather than one enormous one: you build a boiler house, you build a hall, and you plumb them together across your yard. It is also why the §6Heat Recovery Steam Generator§r can exist at all.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum23=The §6Steam Turbine Hall§r is the largest thing in this chapter and the most efficient. Steam in at the condenser end, Flux out at the switchyard.<br>It is slow to start and it hates being cycled. Feed it steadily and nothing in the mod converts fuel to power better; switch it on and off against a wobbling demand and it will spend most of its life spinning up.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum24=The §6Heat Recovery Steam Generator§r burns nothing whatsoever. Butt it against a §6Gas Turbine§r's exhaust end and it makes steam out of heat that was going up the stack regardless.<br>One HRSG claims one turbine's exhaust, so two of them on the same machine is one of them wasted. The face it needs is three wide and three tall, which is exactly the turbine's exhaust face - if it will not sit flush, it is not in line.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum25=Turbine, HRSG, hall. That is the §6combined cycle§r, and it is the point of this whole section: the gas turbine's waste heat becomes steam, the steam becomes more Flux, and the same millibucket of gas does considerably more work than it does alone.<br>It is also the least forgiving thing to build. Three structures in a line, one fuel line, one steam line, and any of the three idle makes the other two worse.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum26=The §6Reciprocating Engine Bank§r is the honest workhorse: diesel, biodiesel or gas, instant response, mediocre efficiency.<br>It is the only plant here that scales by §obuilding another one§r. Put a second bank alongside the first and they run as a set. Nothing about one bank changes - you simply have two - which makes it the plant to build when you are not sure how much power you will need.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum27=A fuel supply should not have to be an eyesore. The §6Tank Fill Cap§r is the only part of a buried tank that shows above ground: a pipe connects to it, a comparator reads it, and its gauge is the whole of the tank's readout.<br>Dig the hole, line it, put the cap in the middle of the roof, backfill, and hammer it. §cA tank that is not covered will not form§r - and it will tell you so rather than simply refusing.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum28=The §6Domestic Tank§r is two by two by two of iron sheetmetal under the garden, holding thirty-two buckets.<br>It is the cheap one on purpose: the first thing to build once you have a fuel worth keeping, rather than the reward for finishing a refinery. A couple of days of heating oil, or enough diesel to keep one generator honest overnight.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum29=The §6Commercial Tank§r is a five by five hole four deep, lined in steel, holding exactly what a Sheetmetal Tank holds.<br>Matching that is the point. This is not a bigger store - it is the same store out of sight, and what it costs is a considerably larger hole.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum30=The §6Bulk Depot§r is nine by nine and six deep, and holds four million millibuckets: a refinery's own stock, or a town's.<br>Nearly eight times the commercial tank for three and a half times the blocks, which is the reason to dig the bigger hole. Its middle is hollow - you are lining an excavation, not filling one.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum31=The §6Gas Station Pump§r hands fuel to a §operson§r. §lStack two of them and strike one with an Engineer's Hammer§r: they become one bowser two blocks tall, with the price on its own panel. Breaking either block takes it apart.<br>Right-click it holding anything that takes fluid - a bucket, a jerrycan, a Mining Drill - and it fills it.<br>Right-click it empty-handed for the panel: what is in the tank, the price per bucket, and how much this pump has ever sold. §oThis fork has no currency§r; the price is a number the pump reports to whatever your server does about money.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum32=Take the §6Fuel Nozzle§r off a pump and it remembers which pump. Then right-click anything within eight blocks that accepts fluid and the pump fills it - a generator across the yard, a tank, a machine. Sneak-right-click the air to rack it again.<br>The §6Forecourt Price Sign§r has no price of its own. It finds the nearest pump and shows that one's, which is the only arrangement that cannot go stale.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum33=§6Gasoline§r runs handheld tools and a Diesel Generator will not touch it, which is correct - a compression engine cannot burn it. The §6Portable Generator§r is what it is for.<br>One block, 256 Flux a tick, four buckets of gasoline or ethanol, about five minutes of running. Carry it to the job, run your lights, walk it back to the pump. It refuses diesel, exactly as the Diesel Generator refuses gasoline.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum34=Filling one jerrycan by hand is fine. Filling eleven is not.<br>The §6Fluid Loading Gantry§r takes empties out of the inventory on one side, fills them, and puts them into the inventory on the other. It has no interface at all - where you put the chests §ois§r the configuration.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum35=The §6Cracking Unit§r breaks the heavy cuts into the light ones. Heavy fuel oil or naphtha goes in at the deck; §6gasoline§r leaves along the front of the head and §6diesel§r along the back, and the row between them connects to nothing at all.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum36=§6The dial is the firebox.§r The cracker always asks for the heat of a maximum-severity run and cracks at whatever severity the heat it actually gets pays for. A burner on heavy fuel oil runs it hot and yields gasoline; one on natural gas runs it cool and yields diesel. Hammer the deck to read what the last pass ran at.<br>Heavy fuel oil also cokes out. §6Petroleum coke§r burns half again as long as coal, which is what stops the byproduct being a nuisance.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum37=A field that still has its own pressure will §cblow out§r if you drill it without a §6Blowout Preventer§r: a fountain of crude, a spill around the wellhead, and a tenth of the field gone up the bore. Right-click the running rig holding one to fit it; the rig warns you on its overlay while there is still time.<br>An §6Absorbent Pad§r lifts spilt fuel three blocks at a time. Spilt crude is flammable and it flows, so do not leave it.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum38=The §6Re-injection Well§r puts water or gas back downhole and gets a second tranche out of a tiring field. It is the thinking player's answer to a flare stack.<br>Water is cheap and poor; scrubbed gas is valuable and good; §6sour gas straight off the wellhead§r sits between them, which means a field can drive its own recovery on the waste it was already producing.<br>§cEach field has a lifetime allowance§r - about a fifth of what it originally held - and it does not come back.
ie.manual.entry.petroleum39=A Core Sample Drill answers "is there oil §ohere§r", one cell at a time, and a cell is a hundred and twenty-eight blocks across. The §6Seismic Survey Kit§r answers "which way do I walk".<br>Right-click the ground holding one - it needs a charge, so carry gunpowder - and it reads the nine cells around you and reports each deposit by bearing and rough range. It never gives you a coordinate. That is still the sample drill's job.

#Virtual fluid network manual
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork.name=Fluid Networks
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork.subtext=Mains without the trench
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork0=A gas main under every road of a town is thousands of pipe blocks, and every one of them is a thing the server has to think about. The §6Virtual Fluid Network§r is the same answer the Virtual Power Grid gives for Flux: named §6mains§r that move fluid between places with no pipe between them at all.<br>It is not only for oil. A main carries whatever you put in it - water, steam, heating oil - and does it at the same cost.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork1=Fluid still has to enter and leave somewhere. It enters at a §6Fluid Inlet§r and leaves at a §6Fluid Outlet§r: service boxes that bolt to any solid surface, including engineering posts.<br>An Inlet draws from whatever it is bolted to - a tank's fill cap, a wellhead - and will also take a pipe run into any of its faces. An Outlet fills whatever it touches, trying the block it is bolted to first.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork2=One §6Fluid Console Housing§r and one each of the three engineering blocks, in a 2x2 wall struck on the face that becomes the screen, make the §6Fluid Control Console§r.<br>Housing top left, §6Redstone Engineering§r beside it, §6Light Engineering§r under the housing and §6Heavy Engineering§r at the bottom right. Hammer any of the four.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork3=The §6Fluid Control Console§r is where mains are made and fittings are assigned. Four tabs: an overview, the mains themselves, the fittings on them, and a minute of throughput history.<br>It runs on a trickle of Flux. Losing power darkens the screen and makes the window read-only rather than refusing to open - being locked out of your own network because the lights are off would be a trap.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork4=§6A main carries one fluid.§r It takes its type from the first Inlet with something to offer, and after that an Inlet holding anything else is simply ignored - which is what stops a mis-plumbed fitting quietly re-typing a live main and feeding every machine on it the wrong thing.<br>To change it, drain the main first. The console will not offer the control until the line pack is empty, and says so.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork5=Each main has caps on what may flow in and out per tick, a small §6line pack§r so fluid collected this tick can be delivered this tick, and optional leakage. The pack is smoothing, not storage - the buried tanks are what storage is for.<br>Outlets marked §6critical§r are served first when supply is short, and a main can name another as a §6backup§r - but only one carrying the same fluid will ever actually cover it.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork7=The §6Fluid Linker§r is the Grid Linker's twin on this side of the plant.<br>Rightclick any fitting with it and a short list of mains appears - name, colour, and what each is doing. Pick one and it links that fitting and loads the tool; every rightclick after that links the fitting you clicked.<br>Sneak-rightclick a fitting to choose a different main, and sneak-rightclick the air to empty the tool. A locked main stays locked.
ie.manual.entry.fluidNetwork6=A §6Main Valve§r is a shut-off one way round and a pressure lamp the other. As a shut-off, a redstone signal holds its main closed; inverted, it demands a keep-open signal instead, which makes it a dead-man's switch.<br>As an indicator it emits while the main is flowing, or - inverted - while it is not, which is a low-pressure alarm. On a real network the fitting that closes a branch and the one that tells you the branch is live are the same thing.

