#JEI description pages for the virtual networks. These are the "what is this for" paragraphs --
#the fine detail lives in the manual, and the tooltip on the block itself carries the live state.
tile.immersiveengineering.grid_device.feed_unit.info=Hands Immersive Flux to a grid segment. String a wire straight to the terminal post on its front, put a cable or connector against it, or bolt it onto a capacitor or generator; then assign it to a segment from a Grid Management Console or with a Grid Linker. Everything a segment can spend comes in through its feed units.
tile.immersiveengineering.grid_device.service_unit.info=Draws Immersive Flux out of a grid segment and pushes it into whatever sits against it, and out along any wire strung to its terminal post. Assign it to a segment from a Grid Management Console or with a Grid Linker. A service unit marked critical keeps its supply when the segment sheds load.
tile.immersiveengineering.grid_device.signal_unit.info=Reads a redstone signal and holds its segment off while the signal is present, or reports the segment's state as a signal of its own. Which of the two it does is set at the console.
tile.immersiveengineering.grid_device.console_housing.info=The terminal of a Grid Management Console. Set it in the top left of a 2x2 wall with a Redstone Engineering Block beside it and Light and Heavy Engineering Blocks below, then strike the face with an Engineer's Hammer. It does nothing on its own.
tile.immersiveengineering.grid_multiblock.grid_console.info=Where the power grid is managed: segments are created, named, coloured and priced here, feed and service units are assigned to them, and schedules and failover are set up. Every change to the grid is made from one of these, which is why it is a structure you walk to rather than something you carry.
tile.immersiveengineering.fluidnet_device.fluid_inlet.info=Feeds a fluid main. It takes fluid from an adjacent tank or pipe and puts it into the main it is assigned to. The first inlet to deliver decides what the main carries; after that the main refuses anything else until it drains.
tile.immersiveengineering.fluidnet_device.fluid_outlet.info=Draws from a fluid main and fills whatever sits against it. Assign it to a main from a Fluid Control Console. An outlet marked critical keeps its supply when the main runs short.
tile.immersiveengineering.fluidnet_device.main_valve.info=Shuts a main by hand or on a redstone signal, and reports whether the main is pressurised. The console can drive one instead of a player.
tile.immersiveengineering.fluidnet_device.console_housing.info=The terminal of a Fluid Control Console. Set it in the top left of a 2x2 wall with a Redstone Engineering Block beside it and Light and Heavy Engineering Blocks below, then strike the face with an Engineer's Hammer. It does nothing on its own.
tile.immersiveengineering.fluidnet_multiblock.fluid_console.info=Where the fluid network is managed: mains are created, named and typed here, inlets and outlets are assigned to them, and schedules and failover are set up. The power grid's console is a separate structure -- the two networks are managed apart because they fail apart.
item.immersiveengineering.network_terminal.info=Shows the power grid and the fluid network from anywhere, and changes neither. Every segment and every main, with what it is doing this tick. Changes are still made at a console.
item.immersiveengineering.network_linker.grid.info=Links grid boxes to a segment without walking back to a console. Rightclick a box to pick a segment from a short list; every rightclick after that links the box you clicked. Sneak-rightclick a box to choose again, or the air to empty the tool. Segment locks still apply.
item.immersiveengineering.network_linker.fluid.info=Links fluid fittings to a main without walking back to a console. Rightclick a fitting to pick a main from a short list; every rightclick after that links the fitting you clicked. Sneak-rightclick a fitting to choose again, or the air to empty the tool. Main locks still apply.

#Conduits
tile.immersiveengineering.conduit.conduit_run.name=Conduit
tile.immersiveengineering.conduit.conduit_run.info=Surface-mounted tubing. It clips flat to whatever face you place it against and runs in that face, turning in right angles -- the indoor counterpart to a wire, which sags. A run wraps around corners by itself: it climbs the wall it reaches, and follows the edge of the beam it is on around onto the next face. An inner corner needs a block in the corner to turn around; an outer one needs nothing.
tile.immersiveengineering.conduit.junction_box.name=Conduit Junction Box
tile.immersiveengineering.conduit.junction_box.info=Where a conduit run ends and a bundle splits. String an LV, MV or HV wire straight to a face, or put a connector of that tier against it, and the box breaks a free conductor out to it -- one wire per face, so a box carries up to six separate circuits and the tier of each is the tier of the hardware on its face. Sneak and hit a face with an Engineer's Hammer to step it through the colours and then to no breakout at all; a dye does it in one hit. Breakouts default to red on the left, blue in the middle and green on the right, and reach the block edge so whatever is bolted against them meets them flush. Right-click a patched face with redstone dust to cycle it between power, reading redstone, and emitting it -- a conductor carrying a signal reaches every box on the run. A face does one of the three, never two, so a run cannot latch itself on. A box passes the whole bundle through whatever is patched, so one dropped in mid-run to turn a corner needs no setting up. Sneak and right-click a face empty-handed to unpatch it.
tile.immersiveengineering.conduit.ground_feeder.name=Conduit Ground Feeder
tile.immersiveengineering.conduit.ground_feeder.info=A hole through a floor or a wall that a conduit run passes straight through, wearing whatever is around it. It surveys its surroundings when placed and puts on the block it finds most of, counting what is visible over what is buried -- one set into a lawn wears grass rather than the dirt underneath. Right-click it with any full block to pin that instead; sneak and right-click it empty-handed to hand it back to the survey. It carries the whole bundle along one axis and splits nothing: an Engineer's Hammer turns that axis. Crossing one is what lets a run change to a surface with no corner between the two; runs turn ordinary corners on their own.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.groundFeeder.auto=Surveying its surroundings again

#Engineer's Manual: conduits
ie.manual.entry.conduits.name=Conduit
ie.manual.entry.conduits.subtext=Wiring that stays on the wall
ie.manual.entry.conduits0=Wire sags. Strung across a valley that is exactly right, and strung along a ceiling it is exactly wrong.<br>§6Conduit§r is the indoor answer: thin tubing that clips flat to whatever face you put it against and runs in that face, turning in right angles. One length of it carries §6sixteen§r independent conductors, named after the sixteen dyes.
ie.manual.entry.conduits1=Conduit is cheap and comes eight at a time. Place it against a wall, a floor or a ceiling and it clips to whatever you clicked.<br>Lengths joined along a surface make a §6run§r, and a run §6wraps around corners§r: it climbs the wall it reaches and follows the edge of the beam it is on around onto the next face. Click whichever face makes sense - the wall ahead of the run, the far side of the edge, or the exposed face of the last length - and you get the piece that turns the corner.<br>An inner corner is turned inside the last length's own block, so it needs something in the corner to turn around; a doorway at the foot of the wall leaves nothing there and the run stops. An outer corner needs nothing at all.
ie.manual.entry.conduits2=A run has to end somewhere, and that somewhere is a §6Junction Box§r. A run arrives at a box face on rather than around a corner, so put the box §6at§r the corner: it sits in the plane of whichever run reaches it and both meet it flush.<br>Lay conduit between two boxes and the run connects itself. There is no coil to craft and no tool to click with: the run is the thing you built, and pulling a length out of the middle of it breaks the connection exactly as you would expect.
ie.manual.entry.conduits3=The box is where a bundle splits.
ie.manual.entry.conduits4=String an §6LV, MV or HV wire§r straight at a face of a junction box, or put a §6Connector§r of that tier - or a §6Grid Feed or Service Unit§r - against one, and the box §6breaks a free conductor out to that face by itself§r, wearing that conductor's plate so you can see which. That is all most circuits need.<br>To choose which conductor, right-click the face with a §6dye§r: the colour you used leaves by that face, and whatever is on it picks up that conductor and nothing else - so one bundle down a corridor can feed a lighting circuit, a workshop and a furnace, each on its own colour.<br>A colour lives on one face at a time. Patching it somewhere new takes it off wherever it was.
ie.manual.entry.conduits9=A junction box is a §6wire endpoint in its own right§r. Point a coil at the face you want and the wire attaches there - no connector bolted to the wall beside it, and nothing to explain.<br>§6One wire per face§r, and the face you clicked is the circuit you get: six faces, six circuits, six conductors. The face the box is bolted to is not one of them, and a box that already has a wire everywhere it can take one will say so.<br>§6The tier of a circuit is the tier of the wire on it.§r An HV wire on one face and an LV wire on another are two circuits of two tiers coming out of the same bundle. Cut one off with §6Wirecutters§r and the face is free for another; the conductor stays patched where it was.<br>The connectors still work. Three of them round a box with the runs buried is what an underground feeder looks like, and nothing about it has changed.
ie.manual.entry.conduits5=A box passes the whole bundle through whether or not anything is patched, so a box dropped in to turn a corner or change surface needs no setting up at all.<br>Sneak and right-click a face empty-handed to unpatch it.
ie.manual.entry.conduits6=Right-click a §6patched§r face with §6Redstone Dust§r and it cycles: power, then reading redstone, then emitting it.<br>A conductor carrying a signal reaches every box on the run, so a lever at one end of a corridor throws a lamp at the other along the same bundle already carrying its power. A face does one of the three and never two - which is what stops a run from latching itself on and refusing to let go.
ie.manual.entry.conduits7=A junction box is the right way to leave a surface indoors, where it reads as hardware. Out in a field it is a steel box sitting in a lawn.<br>The §6Ground Feeder§r is the other answer.
ie.manual.entry.conduits8=Set a feeder into a floor, a wall or the ground and it §6puts on whatever is around it§r - grass in a lawn, stone in a cellar wall. It looks at what is visible rather than at what is merely nearby, so one buried at ground level wears the turf and not the dirt beneath it.<br>A run goes straight through it along §6one axis§r, which an §6Engineer's Hammer§r turns; the conduit on the far side may be clipped to a different surface, and crossing a feeder is what lets a run change to a surface with no corner between the two. It splits nothing - a breakout is a thing you should be able to see.<br>Right-click it with any full block to pin that block instead, or sneak and right-click it empty-handed to let it survey again.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.storage_tank_wall.name=Storage Tank Wall
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.storage_tank_wall.info=Build a hollow box of these -- any rectangular shape from 3x3x3 up to 16x16x16 -- and strike any wall with an Engineer's Hammer. It becomes one tank holding 16 buckets per enclosed block, so a 5x5x5 holds 432,000 mB and a 9x9x9 holds 5,488,000. Walls only: the inside must be empty. Pipe into any wall block, not just one fitting. Hammer it again to take it apart without losing the contents.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.oilfield_frame.info=The structural part every oilfield rig is built out of, and the stack a finished rig packs itself back into.<br><br>None of the oilfield machinery is crafted as a block and placed. It is built in position out of frame, steel and sheetmetal, then struck with an Engineer's Hammer. Keep plenty of it.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.wellhead.info=What a Drilling Derrick leaves standing over a finished bore. From then on it is the whole of the well.<br><br>It buffers a couple of seconds of production and pushes it into anything beside it that accepts fluid. Sour gas comes up dissolved in the crude -- a quarter of a bucket per bucket -- into a second buffer, and a full gas buffer stops the well, oil and all. Give the gas somewhere to go.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.flare_stack.info=Stood against a Wellhead, it burns off the gas nothing else wants.<br><br>It takes gases only -- it will not accept crude, so it is no way to make a spill disappear -- destroys what it is given and hands back nothing. It lights the ground around it while burning, and that light is the entire return. A flare is a loss you accept until you can afford a Gas Scrubber or a Re-injection Well.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.lubrication_manifold.info=Where lubricant goes. Bolt it against any of the mod's larger machines, fill it, and that machine costs noticeably less to run.<br><br>It only draws while its host actually has something on the go, so an idle machine will not quietly empty it. A bucket is about eight minutes of continuous work.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.propane_cylinder.info=A bottle of propane you fill somewhere and carry to wherever it is needed. Holds 4,000 mB.<br><br>Right-click it with a fluid container to fill or empty it. The Upright and Torpedo tanks are the same idea at 12,000 and 24,000 mB.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.propane_tank_upright.info=A standing propane tank holding 12,000 mB -- three cylinders' worth in one block.<br><br>Right-click it with a fluid container to fill or empty it.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.propane_tank_torpedo.info=The largest propane body: a horizontal torpedo tank holding 24,000 mB.<br><br>It stops deliberately short of the buried Domestic Tank's 32,000: above this size, fuel belongs underground. Right-click it with a fluid container to fill or empty it.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.tank_fill_cap.info=The only part of a buried tank that shows above ground. A pipe connects to it, a comparator reads it, and its gauge is the whole of the tank's readout.<br><br>Dig the hole, line it, put the cap in the middle of the roof, backfill, and hammer it. A tank that is not covered will not form.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.gas_pump.info=Hands fuel to a person rather than to a machine. Stack two of them and strike one with an Engineer's Hammer: they become a single bowser two blocks tall, with the price on its own display panel. Breaking either block takes it apart again.<br><br>Right-click it holding anything that takes fluid -- a bucket, a jerrycan, a Mining Drill -- and it fills it.<br><br>Right-click it empty-handed for the panel: what is in the tank, the price per bucket, and how much this pump has ever sold. This fork has no currency; the price is a number the pump reports.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.forecourt_sign.info=A price board for a forecourt. It has no price of its own -- it finds the nearest Gas Station Pump and shows that one's.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.portable_generator.info=One block, 256 Flux a tick, four buckets of gasoline or ethanol, about five minutes of running.<br><br>It is what gasoline is for: a Diesel Generator will not touch gasoline, because a compression engine cannot burn it. Carry this to the job, run your lights, walk it back to the pump. It refuses diesel, exactly as the generator refuses gasoline.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_device.reinjection_well.info=Puts water or gas back downhole and gets a second tranche out of a tiring field. The thinking player's answer to a Flare Stack.<br><br>Water is cheap and poor; scrubbed natural gas is valuable and good; sour gas straight off the Wellhead sits between them -- so a field can drive its own recovery on the waste it was already producing.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.derrick.info=Sinks the bore: a 3x3 lattice nine blocks tall, hammered together like any other structure.<br><br>Power goes into the drill floor -- the whole bottom course takes a connector on any face -- and it wants around 256 Flux a tick for the minute the bore takes. If there is nothing underneath it says so in chat the moment it forms. When the bore is done it hands back its frames and leaves a Wellhead.<br><br>A field that still has its own pressure will blow out if you drill it without a Blowout Preventer.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.pumpjack.info=What a tired well needs. Build it so its well bay lands over an existing Wellhead -- the bay is not a part you supply, and forming the machine will never swallow the well it exists to work.<br><br>Above about three fifths pressure a deposit lifts itself and a pumpjack does nothing and costs nothing. Below that line the well delivers nothing at all without one. Power connects across the motor blocks at the back.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.gas_scrubber.info=Turns flared sour gas into two products: twin vessels six blocks tall on a 3x3 skid, with an alley between them to stand and plumb in.<br><br>Height is the interface. Sour gas enters along the skid at the bottom, sweet natural gas leaves along the head course at the top, and sulfur drops out.<br><br>It runs on heat from a lit Industrial Burner against its skid, not on Flux. Four fifths of the sour gas survives; the missing fifth becomes the sulfur.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.distillation_tower.info=Where crude becomes worth something: a 2x2 steel column twelve blocks tall on a scaffolding deck, with nozzles out of the shell in pairs at seven heights.<br><br>The nozzle heights are the machine. Each pair draws exactly one cut, in column order, lightest at the top: natural gas, naphtha, gasoline, diesel, heavy fuel oil, lubricant, and bitumen at the foot. Point a pipe at the wrong height and you get the wrong fluid, not a mixture.<br><br>Crude goes in at the deck. Heat comes from an Industrial Burner against its side -- beside it, never under it.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.industrial_burner.info=A 3x3 firebox of blast brick under a steel sheetmetal crown, with a burner head where the fuel line lands.<br><br>It makes no Flux at all, and that is the point of it. It makes heat, which is what a Distillation Tower, a Gas Scrubber and a Cracking Unit run on. Stand one against the side of the machine it serves.<br><br>Heavy fuel oil is worth the most to it; diesel, propane and natural gas burn too. It will also heat whatever stands on its crown, ordinary furnaces included.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.cracking_unit.info=Breaks the heavy cuts into the light ones. Heavy fuel oil or naphtha goes in at the deck; gasoline leaves along the front of the head and diesel along the back, and the row between them connects to nothing at all.<br><br>The dial is the firebox. It always asks for the heat of a maximum-severity run and cracks at whatever severity the heat it gets pays for -- a burner on heavy fuel oil runs it hot for gasoline, one on natural gas runs it cool for diesel. Heavy fuel oil also cokes out.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.gas_turbine.info=Burns natural gas or propane for Flux: three blocks high and six long, filter house to nacelle to alternator to exhaust stack.<br><br>Gas goes in anywhere along the bottom course; power leaves through the three cells above the alternator. A redstone signal stops it.<br><br>It does not switch on, it spools up: ten seconds to full output, burning most of its gas from the first tick while producing nothing. Every cold start throws away about three seconds of fuel. Leave it running.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.hrsg.info=Burns nothing whatsoever. Butt it against a Gas Turbine's exhaust end and it makes steam out of heat that was going up the stack regardless.<br><br>One HRSG claims one turbine's exhaust, so two on the same machine is one wasted. The face it needs is three wide and three tall, which is exactly the turbine's exhaust end.<br><br>Turbine, HRSG, hall is the combined cycle, and the point of the whole section.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.fuel_oil_boiler.info=The furnace half of a power station. It makes no Flux at all -- it makes steam.<br><br>That is what finally gives heavy fuel oil a job at scale. Crude and diesel burn in it too and both are worse trades. Water goes in along the base course, fuel goes in with it.<br><br>Steam is a real fluid here: it has a bucket and a pipe, and it moves between buildings. Plumb this to a Steam Turbine Hall across the yard.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.steam_turbine_hall.info=The largest thing in the chapter and the most efficient. Steam in at the condenser end, Flux out at the switchyard.<br><br>It is slow to start and it hates being cycled. Feed it steadily and nothing in the mod converts fuel to power better; switch it on and off against a wobbling demand and it will spend most of its life spinning up.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.engine_bank.info=The honest workhorse: diesel, biodiesel or gas, instant response, mediocre efficiency.<br><br>It is the only plant here that scales by building another one. Put a second bank alongside the first and they run as a set -- nothing about one bank changes, you simply have two. The plant to build when you are not sure what you need yet.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.loading_gantry.info=Filling one jerrycan by hand is fine. Filling eleven is not.<br><br>It takes empty containers out of the inventory on one side, fills them, and puts them into the inventory on the other. It has no interface at all -- where you put the chests is the configuration.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.domestic_tank.info=2x2x2 of iron sheetmetal under the garden, holding thirty-two buckets.<br><br>The cheap one on purpose: the first thing to build once you have a fuel worth keeping, rather than the reward for finishing a refinery. Line the hole, put a Tank Fill Cap in the middle of the roof, backfill and hammer it.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.commercial_tank.info=A 5x5 hole four deep, lined in steel, holding exactly what a Sheetmetal Tank holds.<br><br>Matching that is the point. This is not a bigger store -- it is the same store out of sight, and what it costs is a considerably larger hole.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_multiblock.bulk_depot.info=Nine by nine and six deep, holding four million millibuckets: a refinery's own stock, or a town's.<br><br>Nearly eight times the commercial tank for three and a half times the blocks, which is the reason to dig the bigger hole. Its middle is hollow -- you are lining an excavation, not filling one.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_decoration.asphalt.info=Road. Put 250 mB of bitumen through a Mixer with sand and gravel for 500 mB of wet asphalt, then pour it -- it flows, finds its own level and sets a moment later into a full block.<br><br>No forms and no depth to judge, because a road is flat by definition.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_decoration.asphalt_marked.info=Asphalt with lane markings painted on it. Cosmetic.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_decoration.asphalt_tile.info=A tiled variant of asphalt, for forecourts and yards rather than open road. Cosmetic.
tile.immersiveengineering.petroleum_decoration.forecourt_canopy.info=The roof a filling station stands under. Cosmetic.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.petroleum.tankFormed=Storage Tank formed: %s, %s mB
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerNoRoom=Not enough room -- the Crawler needs a clear five blocks across.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerAttachment=Fitted: %s
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerBucketDumped=Bucket emptied.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerReserve=Running on reserve -- refuel before working.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerNoFuel=No diesel. Right-click the Crawler with a container of diesel to refuel it.
ie.manual.entry.crawler.name=Hydraulic Crawler
ie.manual.entry.crawler.subtext=A machine you climb into
ie.manual.entry.crawler0=The §6Excavator§r on the facing page is a building. This is not.<br>The §6Hydraulic Crawler§r is a tracked machine that arrives as an item, unfolds where you put it, and is §odriven§r. It needs five clear blocks across to unfold into. Right-click it to climb in; there is no GUI, and everything it does is done from the seat.
ie.manual.entry.crawler1=§lDriving.§r §6W and S§r drive the tracks, §6A and D§r steer them. It is heavy: about a second to reach full speed, rather less to stop, and it brakes harder than it accelerates so it does not coast. Reverse is slower, as it is on anything tracked, and it turns tightest standing still. A one-block ledge is climbed rather than bumped into.<br>§lLooking.§r The cab turns to wherever you look, at the speed a slew ring turns. The house follows your head, so turning to face what you are working on is the same gesture as slewing towards it. You cannot look around independently of the machine; that is the trade.
ie.manual.entry.crawler2=§lThe arm.§r It holds its own aim rather than following your view, so you can watch what you are digging while you dig it.<br>§6R and C§r raise and lower it. §6X and Z§r reach out and draw back in. Those are two separate axes, and you need both: elevation alone would put the tool on a single arc, so anything nearer or further would be out of reach wherever you parked.<br>The panel in the corner shows both, and turns §cred§r at the stops.
ie.manual.entry.crawler3=§lThe tools.§r §6G§r changes attachment, §6V§r works it.<br>The §6Bucket§r digs and keeps what it digs. §6Sneak and V§r tips the load out at the machine's feet - there is no other way to unload it by hand, though a pipe or a conveyor against the machine will empty it too. When the bucket fills it drops the rest on the floor rather than eating it.<br>The §6Breaker§r destroys what it touches and leaves it on the ground. The §6Grapple§r picks one block or one creature up and puts it down again.
ie.manual.entry.crawler4=§lDiesel, and nothing else.§r Right-click the machine with a container of §6Diesel§r to fuel it - the refined cut, not Biodiesel. The Gas Station Pump will fill it.<br>The engine burns while anyone is aboard, and the attachment burns more. Below §6400mB§r the tool stops but the tracks keep turning: that reserve exists so the machine cannot strand you a long way from home, in a thing that cannot be pushed.<br>§lTaking it away.§r §6Sneak and right-click with an Engineer's Hammer.§r It comes back as an item, with its diesel still in it.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerWrongFuel=The Crawler runs on Diesel and will take nothing else.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerTankFull=The tank is already full.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerFuelled=Fuelling: %s / %s mB Diesel (%s%%)
desc.immersiveengineering.info.crawlerFuel=Diesel: %s / %s mB
item.immersiveengineering.hydraulic_crawler.info=A tracked machine you climb into and drive. Place it on clear ground, right-click to get in.<br><br>WASD drives, look to slew the cab. R/C raise and lower the arm, X/Z reach in and out, G changes attachment and V works it.<br><br>Runs on Diesel. Sneak and right-click with an Engineer's Hammer to take it away again.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.petroleum.tankDisassembled=Storage Tank taken apart. Its contents are kept.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.petroleum.tankNotAShell=That is not a hollow box. A Storage Tank needs complete walls from 3 to 16 blocks a side, and nothing inside it.
chat.immersiveengineering.info.petroleum.pumpAssembled=Gas Station Pump assembled. Break either block to take it apart again.
